===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who [[deviate]]s, especially from [[norm]]s of social [[behavior]].
#: {{ux|en|He was branded as a '''deviant''' and ostracized.}}
# A [[thing]], [[phenomenon]], or [[trend]] that deviates from an expectation or [[pattern]].
#: {{ux|en|As the graph shows, the March sales trend is the '''deviant'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|DeviantArt member}} {{lb|en|Internet}} A member of the online art community {{w|DeviantArt}}.
#: {{inline alt forms|en|Deviant}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Peter Mechant; Lieven De Marez|chapter=Studying Web 2.0 Interactivity: A Research Framework and Two Case Studies|title=Cyber Behavior: Concepts, Methodologies, Tools, and Applications: Concepts, Methodologies, Tools, and Applications|publisher=Information Science Reference|year=2014|page=1709|isbn=978-1-4666-5943-8|passage=In contrast to Flickr, deviantArt offers a broad range of user affordances, enabling both synchronous and asynchronous interpersonal and group communication, as well as networking with other '''deviants''' and the surveillance of the work of others (through the deviantWATCH affordance).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Grekkikay|title={{noitalic|post on how to critique art}}|year=2009|newversion=quoted in|2ndauthor=Linda Vigdor|chapter2=Constructing Learning through the Creative Evaluation of Visual Arts Production|editor2=Hiesun Cecilia Suhr|title2=Online Evaluation of Creativity and the Arts|series2=Routledge Studies in New Media and Cyberculture|location2=New York, N.Y.; London|publisher2=w:Routledge|year2=2015|section2=section “Creative Evaluation as Practiced in Three Critique Environments”, exhibit 2 (DeviantArt.com)|pages2=87–88|passage=While I myself have no problems as far as favoriting instead of commenting is concerned, since a lot of '''deviants''' are like this, I thought it was right to tell people how to comment ''correctly''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Alkim Almila Akdag Salah; Albert Ali Salah|title=Flow of innovation in deviantArt: following artists on an online social network site|journal=Mind & Society|volume=12|date=22 February 2013|pages=137–149|passage=Our data stem from these DD’s and galleries of the corresponding '''deviants''' and were acquired directly from dA Headquarters.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Brian Lee Jones|chapter=Deviously Deviant: The Strange Tapestry that is deviantART.com|editors=Barbara Guzzetti; Mellinee Lesley|title=Handbook of Research on the Societal Impact of Digital Media|series=Advances in Media, Entertainment, and the Arts|publisher=Information Science Reference|year=2016|page=390|isbn=978-1-4666-8311-2|passage=Many '''deviants''' strongly criticized the sharing features as facilitating art theft (Herr-Stephenson & Perkel, 2008; Perkel, 2011).}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Natt Garun|title=Wix has acquired DeviantArt, which may let artists license their work for the site builder|work=w:The Verge|url=https://www.theverge.com/2017/2/24/14726690/wix-acquired-creative-online-community-deviantart|date=24 February 2017|passage=“'''Deviants''' continue to own their own works,” [[w:Angelo Sotira|[Angelo] Sotira]] wrote. “In the future, there's a possibility Wix might provide opportunities for you to license your work — only if you want to — to more people around the world.”}}

